Q: How to group flattened SQL rows (one user, multiple skills) into a single object with a List&lt;string&gt; in C#? A: <p>you are going to want to create a separate class - skill - and then create a List of skills.</p>
<pre><code>public class skill
{
    public int skillId { get; set; }
    public string skill { get; set; }
}

public List&lt;skill&gt; skills { get; set; }
</code></pre>

Q: Unable to click button from menu shown by clicking a button using Selenium A: <p>There's a simpler way to find these elements. You can find them directly rather than finding the containing element in one step and then the element containing the text in the second step. For example, here's the &quot;Common&quot; button HTML.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;a tabindex=&quot;0&quot; href=&quot;/adopt-me/shop/pet/hot_doggo/18000&quot; class=&quot;_link_1jj7i_9 _noUnderline_1jj7i_1&quot; matchchildren=&quot;false&quot; disabled=&quot;false&quot; nounderline=&quot;true&quot; external=&quot;false&quot;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;_container_rayfx_1 _selected_rayfx_14 _s_rayfx_14&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;_icon_rayfx_71&quot;&gt;
    ...
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Common&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You can see the &quot;Common&quot; string in the P tag. You can just use</p>
<pre><code>//p[text()='Common']
</code></pre>
<p>No need for the parent container. The only time this approach would be a problem is if there is more than one element that matches and in my (somewhat limited) testing, there isn't.</p>
<p>Now to the code...</p>
<p>I kinda went overboard for this answer but this is how I write test code at work. A little extra coding makes things a lot more readable and easier to maintain. I added enums for the different categories: Item type, Properties, and Age. NOTE: I didn't go through all the selections and add every single value in every single category but this should be enough to see the pattern and get you started.</p>
<p>I moved each of the &quot;steps&quot; into a separate function, e.g. <code>select_item_type()</code>, <code>select_properties()</code>, <code>select_age()</code>, and <code>get_price()</code>. This makes it easier to read the code and understand what's being done in each step. It also makes the code easier to maintain because if they add a new Age, you just add it to the enum and you're done.</p>
<p>I also like to add documentation to classes and functions so I can see what they do.</p>
<p>Hopefully this helps.</p>
<p>The working code:</p>
<pre><code>from selenium import webdriver
from selenium.webdriver.common.by import By
from selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions as EC
from selenium.webdriver.support.wait import WebDriverWait
from enum import Enum


class Item_type(Enum):
    '''An enum of valid Item types NOTE: The string values must match EXACTLY the text shown on the site.'''

    COMMON = &quot;Common&quot;
    NEON = &quot;Neon&quot;
    MEGA_NEON = &quot;Mega neon&quot;


class Properties(Enum):
    '''An enum of valid Properties NOTE: The string values must match EXACTLY the text shown on the site.'''

    FLYABLE = &quot;Flyable&quot;
    RIDEABLE = &quot;Rideable&quot;


class Age(Enum):
    '''An enum of valid Ages. NOTE: The string values must match EXACTLY the text shown on the site.'''

    FLARE = &quot;Flare&quot;
    FULL_GROWN = &quot;Full-Grown&quot;
    JUNIOR = &quot;Junior&quot;
    LUMINOUS = &quot;Luminous&quot;
    NEWBORN = &quot;Newborn&quot;
    POST_TEEN = &quot;Post-Teen&quot;
    PRE_TEEN = &quot;Pre-Teen&quot;
    REBORN = &quot;Reborn&quot;
    SPARKLE = &quot;Sparkle&quot;
    SUNSHINE = &quot;Sunshine&quot;
    TEEN = &quot;Teen&quot;
    TWINKLE = &quot;Twinkle&quot;


def get_price():
    '''Returns the price of the currently selected pet.'''

    return wait.until(EC.visibility_of_element_located((By.XPATH, &quot;//p[./span[@itemprop='price']]&quot;))).text


def select_item_type(item_type: Item_type):
    '''Selects the specified Item type.'''

    wait.until(EC.element_to_be_clickable((By.XPATH, f&quot;//p[text()='{item_type.value}']&quot;))).click()


def select_properties(properties: Properties):
    '''Selects the specified Properties.'''

    wait.until(EC.element_to_be_clickable((By.XPATH, f&quot;//p[text()='{properties.value}']&quot;))).click()


def select_age(age: Age):
    '''Selects the specified Age.'''

    wait.until(EC.element_to_be_clickable((By.XPATH, f&quot;//p[text()='{age.value}']&quot;))).click()


url = 'https://starpets.gg/adopt-me/shop/pet/hot_doggo/17996'
driver = webdriver.Chrome()
driver.maximize_window()
driver.get(url)

wait = WebDriverWait(driver, 10)
select_item_type(Item_type.NEON)
select_properties(Properties.FLYABLE)
select_age(Age.TWINKLE)

print(get_price())
</code></pre>
